00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:00.660
Comment response time!

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:04.540
Alright, after a bunch of your comments, I
realize I could have been a bit clearer.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.620
The title of the video was “Why Doesn’t
Light Have Mass?”

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.020
The answer is that it actually might have
it,

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.820
depending on how you define mass.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.760
When most people hear the word “mass”
they think of rest mass,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.930
which light definitely doesn’t have.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:21.290
However, if you’re an experimentalist, then
you only care about what you can measure.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:25.400
In that case, light does have mass, in every
measurable way.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:29.060
Light can add to something’s gravity
and light can give things momentum when it

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:29.720
hits them.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.600
Because it has that silly thing called “relativistic
mass.”

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:38.340
Rest Mass + Kinetic Mass = Relativistic Mass
None of our measuring devices distinguish

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.060
between different types of mass.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.320
To them, mass is just mass.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.980
Anyway, I apologize for the lack of clarity
at the end of the video.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:47.280
Totally my fault.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.520
Hindsight is a [BEEP]!

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.720
Several of you also pointed out that light
slows down in materials.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:52.940
Except you’re wrong!

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.900
Well, again, this depends on your scientific
philosophy.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:00.260
Say you’ve got two lasers, but only one
passes through a block of glass.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:04.300
That one is definitely going to take longer
to travel the length of the block.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.980
So an experimentalist would say the light
is going slower.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:10.200
But a theoretical physicist, like myself,
looks much deeper.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.840
A lot is going on with that light inside the
block.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:16.480
Along the way, the photons of light have to interact with atoms.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:25.320
The light in the block only appears to slow down.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.040
It doesn’t actually slow down.

